<div class="box-example">
<span id="Example_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B3.7D.5C)"></span><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Map:_Chemistry_-_The_Central_Science_(Brown_et_al.)/11:_Liquids_and_Intermolecular_Forces/11.02:_Intermolecular_Forces" class="mt-section"><span id="Example_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B3.7D.5C)"></span><h5 class="box-legend" id="Example_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B3.7D.5C)-21770"><span class="lt-icon-default">Example \(\PageIndex{3}\)</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-21770">Considering CH<sub>3</sub><span class="acronym-ignore">OH</span>, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>, Xe, and (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N, which can form hydrogen bonds with themselves? Draw the hydrogen-bonded structures.</p>

